Yes — based on this broader sample, the LEAF-family apps do appear to share the same resolver convention, but it is not universal across all report*.php files in the org.

What I verified
I found and checked report.php-style files across org code search results, including:

software/LEAF/LEAF_Nexus/report.php
software/LEAF/LEAF_Request_Portal/report.php
software/LEAF-launchpad/src/launchpad/report.php
All three follow the same core pattern:

$_GET['a'] → $action (sanitized)
switch ($action) for a small number of special actions
fallback: if ($action != '' && file_exists("templates/reports/{$action}.tpl"))
render reports/{$action}.tpl
I also saw non-LEAF apps (e.g., HMIS view_vreport.php) that do not use this pattern at all. So this is clearly a LEAF app convention, not an org-wide PHP convention.

Practical conclusion for aliasing (lp_impact + impact)
For apps using this LEAF resolver, aliasing is generally straightforward and low risk:

either add a second template filename (impact.tpl) that reuses/includes lp_impact.tpl, or
add a switch remap (impact → lp_impact) before template fetch.
Because dispatch is filename-convention-based in these files, this usually works without central route config changes.

Important caveat
You still should validate per app for extra action-coupled logic outside report.php (permissions/menu/redirect/logging code). I did not find evidence in this sample that would block aliasing, but I also cannot claim every LEAF app is identical without app-specific scans.
